-- ============================================================================
-- examples/kan_extensions.kan — the construction Kan is named for, IN Kan.
--
--   "The notion of Kan extension subsumes all the other fundamental concepts of
--    category theory."                              — Saunders Mac Lane
--
-- A LEFT Kan extension of a functor  F : A -> D  along  p : A -> B  is the best
-- approximation of F by a functor on all of B: a functor  L : B -> D  with a
-- natural transformation  η : F ⇒ L∘p  that is UNIVERSAL — every competitor
-- (G, γ : F ⇒ G∘p) factors through a mediating  σ : L ⇒ G.  The RIGHT Kan
-- extension is the dual (mapping IN instead of out).
--
-- Everything below TYPE-CHECKS — and in Kan, a type that checks is a theorem.
-- Run it:   kan check examples/kan_extensions.kan
-- ============================================================================
import "../std/kan.kan"
import "../std/unit.kan"
import "../std/category.kan"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. THE FUNDAMENTAL EXAMPLE — extend along the identity, get F back.
--
-- Every functor is its own Kan extension along the identity functor: take
-- L = F, unit = the identity F ⇒ F∘id = F. This is the simplest genuine Kan
-- extension, and it holds for ANY F, in both variances (std/kan.kan proves the
-- general statements `lanAlongId` / `ranAlongId`; here we exhibit concrete,
-- checked instances so you can see them inhabit the Kan-extension TYPES).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- a concrete functor to play with: the identity on the terminal category.
def F : Functor One One = idFunctor One

-- its left and right Kan extensions along the identity — fully concrete values.
def lanId : LeftKanExt  One One One (idFunctor One) F = lanAlongId One One F
def ranId : RightKanExt One One One (idFunctor One) F = ranAlongId One One F

-- the extension really recovers F: the extending functor L is F itself.
def lanId_recovers_F : Id (Functor One One) (fst lanId) F = refl
def ranId_recovers_F : Id (Functor One One) (fst ranId) F = refl

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2. THE UNIVERSAL PROPERTY, COMPUTED.
--
-- A Kan extension is not just a functor — it carries the universal mediating
-- map. We can pull it out of the extension and RUN it: given any competitor
-- (G, γ : F ⇒ G∘p) it returns the factoring σ : L ⇒ G. So the universal
-- property is not merely stated, it computes.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- the mediating map extracted from the left Kan extension.
def lanId_mediator
  : (G : Functor One One)
    -> NatTrans One One F (compFunctor One One One (idFunctor One) G)
    -> NatTrans One One F G
  = snd (snd lanId)

-- feed it the trivial competitor (F itself, with the identity): out comes a
-- genuine natural transformation σ : F ⇒ F. Checked — the property runs.
def lanId_sigma : NatTrans One One F F
  = lanId_mediator F (idNat One One F)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3. "ALL CONCEPTS ARE KAN EXTENSIONS" — stated in Kan.
--
-- The unique functor  A -> 1  (to the terminal category) turns the slogan into
-- checkable definitions: a COLIMIT of F is its left Kan extension along A -> 1,
-- and a LIMIT is the right Kan extension. Kan lets you say this precisely.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- the unique functor from any category to the terminal category One.
def toOne : (A : SmallCategory) -> Functor A One
  = lambda A: ( lambda a: unit
              , ( lambda a b f: unit
                , ( lambda a: refl , lambda a b c g f: refl ) ) )

-- a COLIMIT of F : A -> D is exactly the left Kan extension of F along A -> 1.
def ColimitOf : (A : SmallCategory) -> (D : SmallCategory) -> Functor A D -> U
  = lambda A D F: LeftKanExt A One D (toOne A) F

-- a LIMIT of F is the right Kan extension of F along A -> 1.
def LimitOf : (A : SmallCategory) -> (D : SmallCategory) -> Functor A D -> U
  = lambda A D F: RightKanExt A One D (toOne A) F

-- These are well-formed Kan TYPES — the slogan is not a metaphor here, it is a
-- statement the checker accepts. Inhabiting them for a general A is the theory
-- of (co)limits (needs coends — Kan's next frontier); the identity extensions
-- in §1 are the base cases Kan already builds and verifies. The point stands:
-- the language named for Kan extensions can express, and check, Kan extensions.
